   "text": "Cities around the world are trying to get more people onto buses, trains, and bikes. The reason is simple: cars clog streets, pollute the air, and waste space. A single bus can carry fifty people who would otherwise need fifty cars. Good public transport also helps those who cannot drive, including the young, the old, and the poor.\n\nThe biggest barrier is reliability. If a train is often delayed or a bus is full and skips your stop, people return to their cars. Cities that succeed invest steadily: clean stations, clear maps, and fares that stay affordable. Some have made city centres pedestrian-only, which reduces noise and makes streets pleasant.\n\nCycling is growing fast where safe lanes exist. When a protected lane connects home and work, people switch quickly. But weather and hills still stop many. The answer is rarely one mode; it is a network where bus, train, bike, and foot connect smoothly.\n\nIn the end, public transport is not just about moving people. It shapes what a city feels like.\n\nWhat surprises newcomers is how much city transport is a matter of design, not destiny. Where lanes are safe and signals clear, people cycle even in rain; where they are not, the same person drives. Cost shapes behaviour too: a monthly pass that beats parking turns occasional riders into daily ones. The greenest trip is the one you would take anyway, made easy. Freight is the hidden half — deliveries vans idle more than buses, and rerouting them saves more carbon than a thousand bike commutes. A city that moves people lightly moves its whole economy more lightly, and the street itself becomes a place again, not a river of metal.",
