   "text": "Exercise is often sold as a way to look good, but its quiet prize is how it makes you feel. A short daily workout lifts mood, sharpens focus, and helps sleep arrive at night. You do not need a gym or fancy gear; a brisk walk counts.\n\nThe hard part is starting, and the harder part is continuing. Motivation fades, so build a routine that does not depend on it. Lay out your shoes the night before. Go at the same time, with the same friend. Make it small enough that skipping feels sillier than doing it.\n\nVariety protects the body. Mix cardio for the heart, strength for the bones, and stretches for posture. Rest is not laziness; muscles grow while you recover, not while you train. Listen to pain that stays — a small ache today can become a real injury tomorrow. Move often, move kindly, and the benefits compound for years.\n\nThe mental case for movement is as strong as the physical. A walk mid-task often solves the problem the screen could not, because the mind wanders usefully when the body moves. Exercise is also a rare legal mood drug: cheap, side-effect free, and available to most. The trap of modern life is that it removed movement from the day — elevators, delivery, desks — so we must now schedule what our grandparents did by living. The fix is to stitch it back into life rather than add a separate 'workout' to resent: stairs over lift, a walk meeting, a bike for the short trip. Active living beats gym guilt every time, because it does not depend on the mood that always fails us.",
