   "words": 290,
   "text": "The future is not a place we arrive at but a thing we build daily. Trends point one way — remote work, clean energy, longer lives — yet surprise remains the only sure thing. The wise plan, but the wise also stay loose, because the map rarely matches the ground.\n\nWork will keep changing. Tasks that can be automated will be, and new roles will appear that have no name yet. The safe bet is not a job for life but a skill for life: learn, unlearn, and learn again. Cities will grow and must green or drown; the prospect of climate pressure is real, not sci-fi.\n\nNone of this should scare us into stillness. An uncertain future is also an open one; we still get to shape it. Vote, build, and adapt — small acts tilt the odds. Envision the world you want, then do the next boring step toward it. The future is closer than it looks, and it is partly yours to write.\n\nThe future also belongs to those who prepare in boring ways. The headline risks — climate, AI, polarisation — are real, but the daily acts that blunt them are plain: learn a skill, vote, plant a tree, fix the draft, teach a child. None is heroic; together they tilt the odds more than any speech. The danger is paralysis — the sense that the problem is too big, so why bother — which is exactly the story the problem wants you to believe. Agency is a muscle; used in small moves it grows, and a person who acts, however slightly, is harder to frighten than one who only watches. The coming decades will be written by the sum of such moves, not by fate. We are not passengers on the future; we are, slowly and clumsily, its authors.",
