   "words": 280,
   "text": "Thirty days in, take a breath. You have been meeting English every day — not in a rush, but in small, steady steps. That is exactly how a language settles in: not by cramming, but by showing up. Look back on the month and you will see more than words; you have a routine, a streak, and a habit that did not exist before.\n\nReview is not repetition; it is consolidation. The words you used in a sentence stick far better than the ones you only read. The grammar you caught in a mistake stays longer than a rule you skimmed. So today, do less new and more old: reread a favourite piece, rewatch a familiar clip, write using only words you once found hard.\n\nReflect on what worked. Mornings or nights? Phone or paper? Then keep that. Learning a language is less a test than a practice you never quite finish — and that is fine. You have built a base; now trust it, use it, and let it grow. The next thirty days are easier because you have already begun.\n\nLooking back also shows how much was learned by showing up bored. The days you did not feel like it are often the ones that stuck, because consistency beats enthusiasm every time. A language, like most things worth having, is less a sprint than a slow climb you stop noticing — until one day you read a sign, tell a joke, or follow a song and realise the words are no longer strangers. That quiet arrival is the reward, and it cannot be rushed, only repeated. So keep the routine, keep the streak, and keep it kind; the goal was never perfection but a life a little wider than before. The next thirty days need no grand plan — only the same small step, taken again, because you already know it works.",
