   "text": "Cities are mostly concrete, but a growing number of them are fighting back with green. Rooftop gardens, pocket parks, and tree-lined streets are appearing in places that were once grey. Planners have realised that greenery is not decoration. It is infrastructure.\n\nTrees cool the air and provide shade, which matters as summers get hotter. Parks clean the air and support biodiversity that cities otherwise lose. Most importantly, they help our wellbeing. A short walk in a green oasis lowers stress in ways a screen cannot.\n\nThe best projects are accessible to everyone. A beautiful garden behind a fence helps no one. Cities that carve out space near homes, and keep it open, see more use and stronger neighbourhoods. Some even revitalise old railways into greenways that connect districts.\n\nIf we invest in green space now, future residents will thank us. The return is quiet but real: cooler streets, cleaner air, and calmer minds.",
