   "words": 260,
   "text": "We often treat sleep as optional, something to sacrifice when work piles up. Science disagrees. While we rest, the brain restores itself and consolidates memory. Students who sleep well remember more and think more clearly the next day.\n\nThe body runs on a circadian rhythm, a roughly 24-hour clock set by light. Late-night screens delay it, so we fall asleep later and wake up tired. Caffeine and alcohol worsen the problem, even when we think they help.\n\nSleep deprivation is not a badge of honour. Studies link it to poorer cognitive function, low mood, and even illness. A short nap can help, but it does not replace a full night.\n\nThe fix is ordinary: a regular bedtime, a dark room, and time to wind down. Protect sleep as you would protect a meeting. The rested you is simply better at being you.",
