   "text": "Artificial intelligence is reshaping work. Routine tasks—data entry, basic translation, simple diagnosis—are being automated. Concern is natural: will machines displace us? History suggests a shift, not an end.\n\nIn the past, new tools made some jobs redundant but created others. The difference now is speed. Entire categories can change within a decade. The wise response is not fear but preparation. Workers who reskill stay employable; those who wait may struggle.\n\nCrucially, AI often augments rather than replaces. A doctor with an AI assistant diagnoses faster; a writer with a tool drafts quicker. The complementary model—human judgement plus machine speed—raises productivity. The key is keeping a human in the loop for decisions that matter.\n\nWe must also act ethically. Who is responsible when an algorithm errs? How do we avoid bias? These questions need public answers. Used well, AI is a tool that frees us for the work only people can do.",
