   "words": 258,
   "text": "Roughly a third of food produced worldwide is never eaten. In rich countries, much is wasted at home: we buy too much, cook too much, and bin the rest. In poorer ones, loss happens earlier, in storage and transport where spoilage is common.\n\nThe cost is huge. Wasted food means wasted water, land, and labour, plus methane from landfills. Yet the fix is often simple. Plan meals, control portions, and use leftovers. Check expiry dates and understand that 'best before' is about quality, not safety.\n\nBusinesses can help. Supermarkets can donate surplus instead of dumping it, and apps now rescue unsold items. Composting turns scraps into soil rather than gas. None of this requires new technology, only conscious choices.\n\nSmall acts scale. If millions trim plate waste, the sum is a quieter planet and fuller tables elsewhere.",
