   "words": 252,
   "text": "We often think of volunteering as pure altruism, giving without return. In truth, the exchange is reciprocal. Those who help gain as much as those helped. A regular shift at a shelter builds bonds across social lines that daily life rarely crosses.\n\nThe personal reward is real but quiet. Contributing time, not money, produces a deep sense of fulfilment that shopping cannot. It also trains empathy: standing in another's shoes, even briefly, widens how we see the world. Communities with active volunteers show stronger cohesion; neighbours know each other and step in when needed.\n\nCritics note that volunteering can mask weak public services. That is fair. It should complement, not replace, duty. Yet for the individual, the act remains transformative. Lending a hand is not lost time. It is how strangers become a community, and how a life gains a steadier meaning.",
