   "words": 252,
   "text": "Politeness is not one size fits all. In some cultures, directness signals honesty; in others, it feels rude, and indirectness shows respect. A blunt 'no' may be normal at home yet offensive abroad. The same word can bridge or bruise, depending on the room.\n\nMisunderstandings often start small. Silence, meant as thoughtfulness, is misread as disagreement. Deference to elders, expected somewhere, looks shy elsewhere. Without tact, good intentions offend. The fix is curiosity: learn the etiquette before you land, and read the room as you go.\n\nHumor helps, but carefully; what amuses one group may shock another. When in doubt, err on the gentle side and ask. Most people forgive a polite question far more readily than an accidental slight.\n\nCross-cultural grace is a skill, not a gift. It is built by noticing nuance and choosing words that include rather than divide.",
