   "text": "High-rise living is efficient but lonely. Behind identical doors, neighbours stay strangers, and the ties that once held streets together fray. Belonging, it turns out, is not automatic; it is built in small, repeated meetings.\n\nRebuilding starts with initiative. One resident who organises a clean-up or a shared meal models that participation is possible. Others join, then reciprocate. Mutual aid appears naturally: a lift, a watch on the flat, a borrowed tool. Trust, once frayed, is slowly rekindled.\n\nThe anchor need not be grand. A corner shop, a park bench, a weekly market—places that force gentle, regular contact—do the quiet work of fostering community. Isolation lifts when people are simply seen.\n\nStrong communities are not nostalgia. They are practical insurance: when crisis hits, the connected cope better. We make them, or we miss them.",
