   "text": "A museum is a room where a nation talks to itself. Behind glass, artefacts hold what words forget: a tool, a toy, a letter—each a tangible link to lives we never met. To stand before them is to step back in time without leaving the city.\n\nCurators do quiet, vital work. They choose what to keep and how to tell it, shaping the narrative a society tells about itself. A good exhibit does not merely display; it revives interest and invites reflection on who we are now.\n\nMuseums also fill a void left by lost memory. Relics pass down identity across generations, especially for those cut off from their past. In a fast-changing world, that continuity is a gift.\n\nYet memory is not neutral. What is preserved, and by whom, always reflects a choice. The honest museum shows the cracks, not only the crown.",
