   "words": 244,
   "text": "We fear being alone, yet solitude is not the same as loneliness. Alone can be a restore—a deliberate quiet that crowds out noise and lets thought surface. Many of our best ideas arrive not in meetings but in the shower, the walk, the still hour.\n\nModern life crowds out solitude. Notifications, commutes, and open plans leave little room to introspect. We fill the silence because silence feels like something is wrong. But clarity often needs that very emptiness.\n\nThe skill is to choose it. Carve out time, sit with yourself, and come to terms with what arises. Solitude nourishes the self the way sleep nourishes the body. It is not escape; it is maintenance.\n\nUsed well, being alone is not a lack of company but a return to it—with yourself, the one person you cannot leave.",
