  "reading": {
   "words": 298,
   "text": "Every time you open an app, an algorithm is already working. It has looked at what you watched last night, how long you stayed, and what you skipped after two seconds. From those signals it builds a rough picture of you and fills your feed with more of the same. Nobody types your preferences into a form; the system simply guesses, and then keeps guessing better.\n\nThis is useful, at least at first. A good recommendation saves time: you find a film you enjoy without reading fifty reviews, or a song that matches your mood on a grey morning. Shops use the same idea, and most people quietly accept it because the suggestions are often right. The trouble starts when relevance turns into repetition. If the system learns that you click on angry headlines, it will serve you more angry headlines, and your view of the world slowly narrows into a comfortable bubble. Researchers describe this narrowing as gradual enough to feel like personal taste rather than restriction. Someone who once read six different newspapers may end up with one, without ever consciously making that choice. The system does not argue with your opinions; it simply removes the effort of meeting anything else, and effort is exactly what a wider view requires.\n\nThe fix is not to delete every app. It is to interrupt the pattern on purpose. Search for something outside your usual interests. Follow one account you mildly disagree with. Clear your history now and then and watch how the feed changes. Above all, notice when you are still scrolling twenty minutes after you meant to stop, because that moment is the product working exactly as designed. Algorithms are tools, and like any tool they should serve the person holding them, not the other way round.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "What signals does the algorithm use to build a picture of the user?",
     "a": "What you watched, how long you stayed, and what you skipped quickly.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why do most people quietly accept shopping suggestions?",
     "a": "Because the suggestions are usually right and save them time.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "The word 'narrows' in paragraph 2 means:",
     "a": "becomes smaller or more limited.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main idea of the passage?",
     "a": "Recommendation algorithms are helpful but can trap us, so we should use them deliberately.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards algorithms?",
     "a": "Balanced but cautious: they are useful tools that must serve the user.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
