  "reading": {
   "words": 261,
   "text": "We are told that we live in an age of data, but most of us were never taught how to read it. A headline announces that a habit doubles your risk of a disease, and the sentence sounds terrifying. If the original risk was one in a million, the new risk is two in a million, and the honest headline would be that almost nothing has changed.\n\nAverages cause similar confusion. If nine people earn very little and one earns an enormous amount, the average salary in that room is comfortable, yet nine of the ten are poor. This is why careful writers report the middle value as well as the average. Sample size matters just as much. A survey of fifty people may be interesting, but it cannot describe a country, and yet such surveys are reported every week as though they could. Graphs deserve equal suspicion. An axis that begins at ninety rather than zero turns a small change into a cliff, and a chart covering three months may hide a trend that is obvious over ten years. None of this is technically dishonest, which is precisely the difficulty: the most misleading presentations usually contain no false numbers at all.\n\nThe most common error, however, is confusing correlation with cause. Ice cream sales and swimming accidents rise together, but ice cream does not cause drowning; hot weather causes both. Before accepting any striking number, three questions help: who collected it, how many people were involved, and compared with what? These questions are not sophisticated, but they remove most nonsense.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "What example does the writer use to explain doubled risk?",
     "a": "A risk rising from one in a million to two in a million.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why can an average salary be misleading?",
     "a": "Because one very high figure raises the average while most people remain poor.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "The word 'striking' in the last paragraph means:",
     "a": "very noticeable or surprising.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?",
     "a": "To teach readers simple questions that reveal misleading statistics.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "How does the writer view the three questions?",
     "a": "As simple but powerful enough to remove most nonsense.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
