  "reading": {
   "words": 275,
   "text": "The most useful question about intelligent software is not whether it will replace people, but which parts of a job it should take. Answered carefully, that question produces better results than either extreme. Radiology offers a clear example. Software can examine thousands of images and flag anything unusual, tirelessly and at three in the morning. It has no idea that the patient is frightened, that the previous scan was taken on faulty equipment, or that a shadow matches an old injury described in a letter. The machine finds patterns, while the doctor understands the situation.\n\nTeams that work well together therefore divide labour by strength rather than status. Anything repetitive, high-volume or measurable goes to the software. Anything requiring context, ethical weight or responsibility for the outcome stays with the person. Crucially, the human must be able to disagree, and the system must make disagreement easy rather than embarrassing. Hospitals that have introduced such tools carefully report a further benefit. Junior staff learn faster when a system explains why an image was flagged, because the explanation works as a second opinion available at any hour. Where the software simply announces a conclusion, the same juniors learn nothing and gradually stop thinking about the case.\n\nThe main danger is not rebellion by machines but excessive deference by people. Studies of automated systems show that operators gradually stop checking results that are usually correct, and then miss the rare occasion when they are not. Good design fights this tendency by explaining its reasoning, indicating uncertainty and requiring genuine human confirmation. Collaboration works when each side does what it does best and neither pretends to be the other.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "What can radiology software do particularly well?",
     "a": "Examine thousands of images tirelessly and flag anything unusual.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "What does the machine fail to understand in the radiology example?",
     "a": "Context such as the patient's fear, faulty equipment or an old injury.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "The word 'deference' in the last paragraph means:",
     "a": "accepting another's judgement without questioning it.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's main argument?",
     "a": "Work should be divided by strength, with humans keeping context and responsibility.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "What does the writer consider the real danger?",
     "a": "That people trust automated results too readily and stop checking.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
