   "prompt": "Some people believe that employers should choose staff mainly on work experience rather than academic qualifications. To what extent do you agree or disagree?",
   "outline": [
    "开头：改写题目并给出立场（部分同意，经验更重要但不能忽视学历）",
    "主体一：经验证明实际能力，简历上的量化成果比分数更能预测表现",
    "主体二：某些职业（医学、工程、法律）必须依靠正式资格保证安全",
    "结论：理想做法是以经验为主、以资格为门槛，两者结合"
   ],
   "model": "Many employers now say that what a candidate has actually done matters more than where they studied. I largely agree with this view, although qualifications remain necessary in certain fields.\n\nExperience is a better guide to future performance. A person who has managed a budget or handled angry customers has already proved that they can do the work under pressure, while a good grade only proves that they can pass an exam. Employers can also check these claims by asking for numbers and references, so experience is easy to test.\n\nHowever, some professions cannot rely on experience alone. Doctors, engineers and lawyers make decisions that affect public safety, and formal qualifications guarantee a shared standard of knowledge. Nobody wants a surgeon who simply learned by watching.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe companies should treat qualifications as a basic entry requirement and then judge applicants mainly on what they have achieved. This balance protects standards without ignoring talented people who learned their skills at work rather than at university.",
