  "reading": {
   "words": 287,
   "text": "Reading faster is a poor goal. Reading appropriately is a better one, because different texts deserve completely different speeds. A contract, a poem and a news article should not be treated in the same way, yet many people read everything at one steady pace and then wonder why they remember so little. The habit usually comes from school, where every text was equally important because every text might appear in the examination.\n\nEfficient readers begin by deciding a purpose. Are you looking for one fact, forming a general opinion, or preparing to be examined? If you need a single number, scan for it and close the page; there is no prize for reading the paragraphs around it. If you need the argument, skim the introduction, the first line of each paragraph and the conclusion, which in most non-fiction gives you eighty per cent of the content in a fifth of the time. Only when a text is genuinely difficult and genuinely important should you slow down and read every word.\n\nThe second habit is deciding what to keep. Highlighting is popular because it feels active while requiring no thought, and a page painted in yellow is no more useful than a clean one. Writing a single sentence at the end of each section, in your own words, forces the mind to select. It is slower on the day and far quicker in the month before an exam, when a page of your own summaries replaces three hundred pages of someone else's prose. Reading well, in other words, is mostly a series of decisions about what deserves your attention and what can safely be left behind.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "What three purposes for reading does the writer mention?",
     "a": "Looking for one fact, forming a general opinion, or preparing for an exam.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why does the writer criticise highlighting?",
     "a": "It feels active but requires no thought, so it does not help selection or memory.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the central argument of the passage?",
     "a": "Match reading speed and method to your purpose, and summarise in your own words.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "The word 'prose' in the final sentence means:",
     "a": "Ordinary written text, as opposed to poetry.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "How does the writer view the goal of reading faster?",
     "a": "Negatively; he considers it a poor goal compared with reading appropriately.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
