  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people think that students should be given printed notes by their teachers, so that they can listen instead of writing. Do you agree or disagree?",
   "outline": [
    "开头：改写题目，立场为不同意或部分同意",
    "主体一：自己做笔记的价值——选择即思考，提高记忆与专注",
    "主体二：讲义的合理用途——补充细节、照顾学习障碍学生",
    "结论：教师可提供大纲与数据，但学生仍应记录自己的理解与问题"
   ],
   "model": "Some educators argue that handing out complete notes allows students to concentrate on the lecture itself. Although this practice has certain benefits, I believe it removes an important part of learning.\n\nTaking notes is not simply copying. A student who decides which points to record is constantly judging what matters, and this act of selection is what creates memory. Research comparing students who write their own summaries with those given printed sheets consistently favours the first group, particularly in later tests. Students who receive everything also tend to stop listening actively, since the material appears to be safely on paper.\n\nThere are, however, situations where printed material is valuable. Complex diagrams, statistics and long quotations are easy to copy incorrectly, and students with hearing difficulties or dyslexia may be unable to write at speed without losing the argument.\n\nA reasonable compromise is therefore for teachers to provide an outline and any detailed data, while students record explanations, examples and their own questions. This approach protects accuracy without removing the thinking that makes a lecture worthwhile.",
   "checklist": [
    "✔ 立场清楚并有让步",
    "✔ 主体段有研究或例证支持",
    "✔ 使用定语从句至少三处（who / which）",
    "✔ 使用 however / therefore 等衔接",
    "✔ 词数 150-200，结论提出折中方案"
   ]
  },
