   "model": "Rising levels of work-related stress have led to a debate about who should deal with the problem: the employer or the employee.\n\nThere is a strong argument that companies must take the lead. Organisations decide workloads, deadlines, staffing levels and management style, and no amount of personal discipline can rescue someone who has been given the work of two people. Employers also have a financial interest in prevention, since exhausted staff make expensive mistakes and eventually leave, and in many countries they already have legal duties regarding health and safety.\n\nOn the other hand, individuals control important parts of the situation. Choosing to stop reading messages at night, taking a full holiday, exercising and asking for help early are personal decisions, and some employees create their own pressure by refusing to delegate or by treating every request as urgent.\n\nIn my opinion, the responsibilities are different rather than shared equally. Companies must fix structural causes such as unrealistic targets, while individuals must maintain the daily boundaries that protect their own recovery. Neither side can solve the problem alone.",
