   "model": "Traditional customs such as tea ceremonies are seen by some people as slow and old-fashioned, while others regard them as an essential part of cultural identity. Both views deserve attention.\n\nThose who criticise such customs point to the pace of modern life. A ceremony that lasts an hour is difficult to fit into a working day, and many young people simply buy a bottled drink instead. From this perspective, keeping complicated rituals alive can feel artificial.\n\nOn the other hand, these traditions are valuable precisely because they are slow. They teach patience, they are passed down between generations, and they create a calm space in which families and friends can talk without screens. A culture that abandons them loses more than a recipe.\n\nIn my opinion, the answer is adaptation rather than rejection. Shorter, simpler versions of traditional ceremonies can survive in modern homes, allowing people to enjoy both convenience and heritage.",
