   "words": 274,
   "text": "The best meal I ate last year cost less than two pounds and I ate it standing up. That is the strange promise of street food: the gap between price and pleasure is wider here than anywhere else in the food world. A vendor who cooks one dish, all day, for thirty years will almost certainly cook it better than a restaurant kitchen preparing forty different items. Specialisation, not equipment, is the secret.\n\nStreet food also tells you more about a city than any guidebook. The stalls appear where people actually live and work, near stations, markets and construction sites, and their prices are set by what ordinary workers can afford. Walk through a bustling night market and you can hear which languages are spoken in the neighbourhood, see which vegetables are in season, and watch office workers and taxi drivers queue side by side at the same plastic tables. Prices tell their own story. When the same bowl of noodles costs twice as much three streets away, you have crossed an invisible border into a wealthier district, and the change is usually visible in the shoes of the people eating rather than in the buildings around them. None of this appears on a map.\n\nThe obvious concern is hygiene. Some visitors avoid street food completely, imagining that anything cooked outdoors must be unsafe. In practice, the busiest stalls are usually the safest, because high turnover means nothing sits around for long and the cooking is done in front of you. The real risk is not the food but the traveller who eats six unfamiliar dishes in one evening and then blames the vendor.",
