  "speaking": {
   "card": "Describe a festival that is important in your country. You should say: when it happens; what people do; what food is eaten; and explain why it matters to people.",
   "part3": "Why are traditional festivals important? / How have festivals changed in the last thirty years? / Should governments spend money on cultural celebrations? / Do young people value traditions as much as older generations?",
   "useful": "It is by far the biggest celebration of the year / The whole family gets together / It has been handed down for generations / The colour red symbolises good luck / People look forward to it all year"
  },
  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people believe that traditional festivals are losing their meaning and have become mainly commercial events. To what extent do you agree or disagree?",
   "outline": [
    "首段：改写题目并表明立场（部分同意）",
    "主体段1：同意——商业化明显，广告、购物与消费取代原本意义",
    "主体段2：不同意——核心的家庭团聚与情感功能仍然存在",
    "结论：商业外壳之下核心未变，教育可帮助保留意义"
   ],
   "model": "It is often claimed that traditional festivals have been reduced to shopping opportunities. I partly agree, since commercial pressure is undeniable, but I do not believe the deeper meaning has disappeared.\n\nThere is clear evidence of commercialisation. Shops begin advertising months in advance, and families feel obliged to buy expensive gifts, decorations and food. For children, the festival may become mainly a list of presents, and the religious or historical origins are rarely explained.\n\nOn the other hand, the essential purpose of most festivals survives. People still travel long distances to eat with their families, cook dishes they never make at other times of year and remember relatives who have died. These actions cost little and have nothing to do with shopping.\n\nIn my view, therefore, commercialisation is a surface change rather than a fundamental one. If schools and parents continue to explain why a festival exists, its meaning will remain intact even in a consumer society.",
   "checklist": [
    "首段表明部分同意的立场",
    "两个主体段分别支持与反驳",
    "至少使用一处过去进行时或过去时叙述细节",
    "结论提出保留意义的具体方式",
    "避免重复使用 very 与 many"
   ]
  },
