   "model": "Public money is limited, and governments must decide between cultural institutions and essential services. Both sides of this debate have reasonable arguments.\n\nThose who favour hospitals and schools argue from urgency. A person waiting for an operation has a more immediate need than a person waiting for an exhibition, and health spending benefits every citizen regardless of education or interest. When budgets are cut, culture is understandably the first item questioned.\n\nSupporters of museums, however, point out that culture is not a luxury. Museums preserve national history, provide free education for schoolchildren, and attract tourists whose spending helps fund the very services mentioned above. The sums involved are also comparatively small, often less than one per cent of national budgets.\n\nIn my view, healthcare must come first, but this should not mean abandoning museums. A balanced solution is partial public funding combined with sponsorship and modest admission fees, so that cultural institutions survive without competing directly with hospitals.",
