  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people say that taking photographs prevents us from truly experiencing a moment. To what extent do you agree or disagree?",
   "outline": [
    "首段：改写题目并表明立场（部分同意）",
    "主体段1：同意——忙于拍照会分散注意力，社交媒体加剧表演性",
    "主体段2：不同意——摄影促使我们更仔细观察，并保存记忆",
    "结论：关键在于数量与意图，而非摄影本身"
   ],
   "model": "It is often argued that constantly photographing an event prevents people from experiencing it properly. I partly agree with this claim, although I believe the problem lies in how we take photographs rather than in photography itself.\n\nThere is certainly evidence for the criticism. At concerts and famous landmarks, many visitors spend more time arranging a shot for social media than looking at what is in front of them. Research also suggests that people who photograph everything remember fewer details, because the camera does the remembering for them.\n\nOn the other hand, taking photographs can sharpen attention. In order to make a good image, you must notice the light, the shapes and the expression on someone's face, which requires looking more carefully than usual. Photographs also preserve moments that memory would otherwise lose entirely.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe photography damages experience only when it becomes automatic. Taking three considered pictures and then putting the camera away allows us to enjoy both the moment and the memory.",
   "checklist": [
    "首段表明部分同意并说明理由方向",
    "两个主体段观点对立且各有论据",
    "至少使用两处动名词或不定式结构",
    "结论提出条件性判断",
    "词数 150–200，无口语缩写"
   ]
  },
