   "model": "Many modern offices now offer yoga or meditation sessions, and people disagree about whether this is the employer's job.\n\nThose in favour argue that office work itself creates the problem. Employees who sit for nine hours develop back pain and rising stress levels, so companies that provide a weekly class are simply repairing damage they help to cause. Such programmes are also cheap, and firms report fewer sick days as a result.\n\nOpponents reply that adults should manage their own health. Not everyone enjoys yoga, gym space is expensive in city centres, and money spent on classes might be better given as salary, allowing staff to choose their own activities.\n\nPersonally, I believe employers should offer opportunities without making them compulsory. A quiet room, a subsidised class, and permission to leave the desk for twenty minutes cost very little, yet they signal that wellbeing matters. The final responsibility remains with the individual, but a supportive environment makes healthy choices far easier.",
