   "model": "Drowning remains one of the most common causes of accidental death among children, which is why some governments want swimming lessons in every primary school.\n\nSupporters of compulsory lessons make a strong safety argument. Swimming is not merely a sport but a survival skill, and school is the only place where children from poorer families will ever receive proper instruction. Once learned, the ability lasts a lifetime.\n\nOthers prefer choice. Building and heating pools is expensive, and in rural areas pupils may travel an hour for a single lesson. Some children are also frightened of water or come from families with cultural objections, and forcing them may create lasting anxiety.\n\nMy own view is that a minimum standard should be compulsory while the rest remains optional. Every child should learn to float, breathe calmly and reach the side of a pool. Beyond that basic level, pupils can decide whether swimming becomes their sport or simply a skill they were fortunate enough to acquire.",
