   "text": "Something quiet has been happening on the streets of European cities. Over the past decade, Paris has built hundreds of kilometres of protected cycle lanes, Seville turned a car-dominated centre into a cycling city in under five years, and even rainy Manchester now sees office workers arriving with helmets under their arms. Cycling has stopped being a hobby for sporty men in tight clothing and has become, once again, ordinary transport.\n\nThe reasons are practical rather than romantic. In dense city traffic, a bicycle is often the fastest way to travel five kilometres, and it never queues for a parking space. Costs are small, emissions are zero, and thirty minutes of pedalling counts as the daily exercise that doctors recommend. Cities benefit too: every commuter who leaves a car at home reduces congestion for everyone who genuinely needs to drive.\n\nThe main obstacle is fear, and it is not irrational. Where cyclists must share space with lorries, accident rates rise and beginners stay at home. This is why infrastructure matters more than campaigns telling people to be brave. Protected lanes, safe junctions and secure parking turn cycling from a sport into a choice available to a sixty-year-old teacher or a parent with two children. Copenhagen did not become a cycling capital because Danes are unusually fit; it built the lanes and the people followed. Politicians who complain that nobody cycles in their city have usually never given them a safe place to ride.",
