   "model": "Popular national parks now receive millions of visitors a year, and managers must decide whether to control access.\n\nThose who support limits focus on damage. Crowded paths become wide muddy scars, litter accumulates faster than rangers can remove it, and nesting birds abandon areas full of noise. A daily quota, they argue, protects the very landscape that visitors come to admire.\n\nOpponents see access as a right. The countryside belongs to everyone, and booking systems tend to favour organised, wealthier tourists who plan holidays months ahead, while local families making a spontaneous Sunday trip are turned away.\n\nI believe a targeted approach works best. A small number of fragile routes, such as famous summits, could require a free permit booked online, while the rest of each park stays completely open. Combined with better paths, more rangers and honest information about quieter alternatives, this would spread visitors out rather than shutting them out, protecting nature without turning the outdoors into a luxury.",
