   "model": "As health budgets come under pressure, some economists suggest paying for gym memberships rather than building more hospital beds.\n\nThe argument for prevention is financially attractive. Regular resistance and aerobic exercise reduces diabetes, heart disease and falls among older adults, all of which are extremely expensive to treat. A subsidised membership costs a fraction of a single week in hospital, and it particularly helps low-income families who cannot afford private facilities.\n\nThe opposing view is more immediate. Hospitals in many countries are already short of nurses and equipment, and patients waiting for surgery cannot be told that the money went to exercise classes. Critics also note that subsidies are often claimed by people who would have exercised anyway.\n\nMy position is that a small, targeted share of the budget should fund exercise. Rather than free membership for everyone, doctors could prescribe supervised strength programmes for patients with specific risks, such as low bone density. This keeps the main budget in hospitals while treating exercise as what it really is: a cheap and effective form of medicine.",
