  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people say modern technology has destroyed our ability to concentrate. Others believe the problem is how we use it, not the technology itself. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.",
   "outline": [
    "引入：注意力下降是否源于科技",
    "科技有害论：通知设计、无限滚动、多任务切换成本",
    "使用方式论：工具本身中立、可设边界与专注模式",
    "立场：设计与个人习惯共同负责，需双向改变"
   ],
   "model": "Complaints about shrinking attention spans are everywhere, and technology usually takes the blame.\n\nThose who blame devices point to design. Applications are built to interrupt, using notifications, endless feeds and rewards that arrive unpredictably. Research on task-switching shows that recovering focus after an interruption can take several minutes, so a worker checking messages every ten minutes may never reach deep concentration at all.\n\nOthers argue that the tools themselves are neutral. The same phone can host a distracting game or a meditation timer, and people who set boundaries, such as disabling notifications and working in fixed blocks, concentrate perfectly well. From this perspective, weak habits rather than wicked engineers are the real problem.\n\nMy view is that responsibility is shared. Individuals should be intentional about when and how they connect, but it is unrealistic to expect ordinary users to out-think teams of designers paid to capture their attention. Alongside personal discipline, we need better defaults: quieter notifications, honest usage statistics, and workplaces that respect the right to switch off.",
   "checklist": [
    "双方立场是否有清晰对比",
    "should / ought to / had better 至少各用一次",
    "主题词汇：autopilot, judgement, intentional, distraction",
    "结论提出个人与制度双层建议",
    "字数 150-200，逻辑连贯"
   ]
  },
