   "text": "Sit up straight. Most of us heard this instruction as children and ignored it, and physiotherapists now say the advice was only half right. Research over the last decade has weakened the idea that there is one perfect posture whose loss explains modern back pain. Studies comparing people with and without back pain often find no clear difference in how they sit. What matters far more is duration: any position becomes harmful if you hold it for four hours without moving.\n\nThis has changed professional advice. Instead of teaching a rigid ideal, clinicians now recommend variety, sometimes summarised as the best posture is your next one. Tissues need load and movement to stay healthy, and a body held perfectly still, even in textbook alignment, becomes stiff and painful anyway. The standing desk that seemed revolutionary a decade ago follows the same rule; standing all day simply produces a different set of complaints.\n\nSensible adjustments remain worthwhile. Raising a laptop to eye level prevents hours of neck flexion, and a chair that supports the lower back reduces fatigue. Beyond equipment, the cheapest intervention is a timer. Two minutes of walking, shoulder rolls or a few squats every hour costs almost nothing and interrupts the accumulation of stiffness. Employers who buy expensive chairs but expect staff to remain at them without pause have solved the wrong problem. The chair is not the enemy; immobility is.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "What do studies comparing people with and without back pain often find?",
     "a": "No clear difference in how they sit.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why is a standing desk not a complete solution?",
     "a": "Because standing all day is another static position and creates different problems.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main argument of the passage?",
     "a": "Movement and variety matter more than achieving one perfect posture.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "The word 'immobility' means:",
     "a": "The state of not moving.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What does the writer think of employers who only buy good chairs?",
     "a": "They have solved the wrong problem by ignoring movement.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
