  "speaking": {
   "card": "Describe a time when you had to recover from an illness or injury. You should say: what happened; how long it took; what helped you; and explain what you learned from the experience.",
   "part3": "Why do people return to sport too early? / Should physiotherapy be free? / How can injuries be prevented? / Is professional sport too demanding on the body?",
   "useful": "take it slow / build up the load / listen to your body / be back on your feet / there is no quick fix"
  },
  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people believe professional sports organisations should be responsible for athletes' long-term health. Others say athletes accept the risks themselves. Discuss both views and give your opinion.",
   "outline": [
    "引入：退役运动员长期伤病问题受关注",
    "机构有责：训练与比赛强度由俱乐部决定、商业收益巨大",
    "个人选择论：成年人自愿签约并获高薪",
    "立场：机构应承担退役后医疗与长期随访"
   ],
   "model": "Former professional players frequently suffer from joint damage and chronic pain decades after retirement, which raises questions about responsibility.\n\nOne view is that clubs and federations must answer for these injuries. They design training loads, schedule matches and profit enormously from players competing while injured. When a young athlete is told that missing a game may cost a contract, the choice is not truly free, so the organisation that created the pressure should fund treatment for as long as the damage lasts.\n\nThe opposing argument emphasises consent. Professional athletes are adults who sign contracts knowing the risks, earn far more than most workers and could choose another career. Extending lifelong medical liability to clubs might also make smaller organisations unable to afford teams at all.\n\nIn my opinion, responsibility should follow influence. Because governing bodies control schedules, medical staff and return-to-play decisions, they should also finance long-term care and independent medical checks. Athletes can accept ordinary risk, but they cannot be expected to accept a system that rewards playing through injury and then abandons them once they are no longer useful.",
   "checklist": [
    "是否给出双方并归结到影响力与责任对应",
    "时间状语从句中未误用 will",
    "主题词汇：rehabilitation, load, relapse, setback",
    "有具体机制说明（赛程、医疗决定）",
    "字数 150-200，衔接紧凑"
   ]
  },
