   "model": "Several countries have introduced school phone bans, and early results have made the policy popular with head teachers.\n\nSupporters cite classroom evidence. Studies in Britain and Norway found improvements in test results after bans, with the largest gains among lower-achieving pupils. Break times also become more social, and cyberbullying during school hours becomes far harder. For teachers, a clear rule is simpler to enforce than constant negotiation.\n\nOpponents raise practical and educational objections. Parents want to contact children about changed arrangements, and pupils travelling alone may genuinely need a phone. More importantly, banning a device teaches nothing about using it wisely, and students will face unlimited access the moment they leave school.\n\nMy own conclusion is that the answer should depend on age. In primary schools phones should be locked away entirely, since young children cannot reasonably be expected to self-regulate. In secondary schools, phones could be prohibited in lessons but permitted at lunchtime, combined with lessons on attention, privacy and algorithms. Never should a school pretend that a ban alone prepares young people for a world designed to capture their attention.",
