   "model": "When cities receive sports funding, they must decide between prestigious stadiums and modest community facilities.\n\nThose who favour community clubs point to participation. A single stadium may host thirty events a year, whereas the same money could keep dozens of pitches, pools and running groups open every week. Since inactivity is a major cause of chronic disease, funding that gets ordinary residents moving produces measurable public health savings. Community clubs also create the social ties that keep people exercising for years.\n\nSupporters of large venues argue differently. Major stadiums attract tournaments, tourism and investment, and international competitions can inspire children to take up sport. A city without such facilities, they claim, loses status and income.\n\nIn my opinion, the balance should favour local provision. Evidence for the so-called inspiration effect is weak: viewing figures rise during major events, but participation rarely follows. Therefore, I would fund community clubs first and require any large project to demonstrate genuine everyday use by residents afterwards. A stadium that stands empty for fifty weeks a year is an expensive photograph; a busy local pitch changes how a neighbourhood lives.",
