   "model": "A growing number of European cities have removed private cars from their historic centres, and the results are increasingly well documented. In my view, the advantages of such schemes clearly outweigh the drawbacks, provided that alternatives are in place first.\n\nThe benefits go beyond cleaner air, although that alone is significant. Cities such as Pontevedra in Spain have reported sharp falls in nitrogen dioxide together with a dramatic reduction in road deaths. Streets without traffic are quieter and safer, children can walk to school unaccompanied, and the space once used for parking becomes cafes, cycle lanes and trees. Retailers often oppose these schemes at first, yet pedestrian areas usually attract more visitors who stay longer.\n\nThe disadvantages should not be dismissed. Elderly or disabled residents may depend on a car, deliveries become more complicated, and traffic can simply move to the streets around the restricted zone, transferring the problem to poorer neighbourhoods. Small businesses that rely on customers arriving by car may genuinely suffer during the transition.\n\nOn balance, however, these problems can be managed with exemptions for residents and disabled drivers, timed delivery windows and serious investment in buses and trams. The health benefits are large, permanent and shared by everyone, while most of the costs are temporary and can be reduced by good planning.",
