   "prompt": "Some people think that protecting rare animals is a waste of money that could be spent on human problems. To what extent do you agree or disagree? (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "Intro: 改写题目，表明不同意这种对立看法。",
    "Body 1: 反驳 - 生态系统提供授粉、水源与药物，保护物种即保护人类利益。",
    "Body 2: 让步 - 承认资源有限，应优先保护栖息地而非单一明星物种。",
    "结论: 保护自然与解决人类问题并不冲突，反而互相支撑。"
   ],
   "model": "It is sometimes argued that money spent saving rare species would be better used on hospitals or housing. I disagree with this view, although I accept that conservation budgets must be spent intelligently.\n\nThe central weakness of the argument is that it treats humans and nature as separate. Around a third of the food we eat depends on insect pollination, coastal wetlands protect cities from flooding, and a large proportion of modern medicines originally came from wild organisms. When a species disappears, we do not simply lose an attractive animal; we lose part of a system that provides services we would otherwise have to pay for. Restoring a damaged river is invariably more expensive than protecting a healthy one.\n\nNevertheless, the criticism contains a fair point about priorities. Enormous sums are sometimes spent on a single famous animal while the habitat around it continues to shrink. Protecting a forest or wetland is usually cheaper and benefits thousands of less glamorous species at the same time, along with the people who depend on that land for water and food.\n\nIn conclusion, I do not believe conservation competes with human welfare; in most cases it supports it. The sensible approach is not to spend less, but to focus on protecting whole habitats rather than individual species chosen for their popularity.",
