   "model": "There is little doubt that the world must move away from coal, oil and gas, and renewable energy is now the cheapest way to generate new electricity. However, I only partly agree with the idea that fossil fuels should be abandoned immediately, because a sudden transition would create problems of its own.\n\nThe case for rapid investment is strong. Solar and wind costs have collapsed over the last decade, so new renewable capacity is often cheaper than a new gas station. Countries that rely on imported fuel also gain energy security, and every year of delay locks in emissions that will affect the climate for centuries.\n\nNevertheless, an immediate halt is not realistic. Wind and sunshine are intermittent, and current storage capacity cannot yet cover long, still winter periods, so gas plants still balance the system. Some industries, such as steel and cement, have no proven low-carbon alternative at scale. A sudden shutdown would also raise electricity prices sharply and destroy jobs in mining regions, which would turn public opinion against the whole transition.\n\nIn my view, the sensible approach is a firm timetable rather than an instant ban. Governments should set legal dates for closing coal stations, invest heavily in storage and grid connections, and fund retraining in affected communities. Moving fast is essential, but moving carelessly would slow progress in the end.",
