  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people think that clothing companies should be legally responsible for the waste their products create. To what extent do you agree or disagree? (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "Intro: 改写题目并表明基本同意。",
    "Body 1: 支持理由 - 生产者责任制已在电子与包装行业奏效，可促使企业改进设计。",
    "Body 2: 潜在问题 - 成本转嫁给消费者、跨国供应链难以监管。",
    "结论: 同意但需国际协调与清晰标准。"
   ],
   "model": "The clothing industry produces enormous quantities of waste, and it is increasingly suggested that brands, rather than councils or consumers, should pay for dealing with it. I largely agree with this principle, though it must be applied carefully.\n\nThe strongest argument concerns incentives. At present, a company profits from selling a garment and bears no cost when it is thrown away six months later, so there is no financial reason to make clothes durable. Extended producer responsibility has already changed behaviour in other sectors: since packaging and electronics firms were charged for disposal, both have become measurably easier to recycle. Applying the same rule to textiles would push brands towards single-fibre fabrics, stronger seams and repair services.\n\nThere are, however, practical objections. Companies would inevitably pass some of the cost to shoppers, and the increase would fall hardest on low-income families who rely on cheap clothing. Supply chains also cross many borders, so a brand selling in one country may manufacture in three others, making enforcement complicated and easy to avoid.\n\nOverall, I support the idea, provided that rules are coordinated internationally and that fees are calculated according to how repairable and recyclable a garment is. A flat charge would simply raise prices, whereas a well-designed system would reward the companies that make clothes worth keeping.",
   "checklist": [
    "✔ 首段改写题目并给出立场",
    "✔ 支持段引用其他行业的先例",
    "✔ 反对段承认成本与监管难题",
    "✔ 使用 however, overall, provided that 等表达",
    "✔ 结论提出具体的设计原则"
   ]
  },
