   "model": "Levels of consumption differ enormously between countries, and it is often argued that wealthy nations should use less so that poorer ones can develop. I broadly agree with this position, although the argument needs to be stated carefully.\n\nThe case rests on simple arithmetic. If the entire global population consumed at the rate of the average North American, several planets would be required, yet only one exists. Rich countries also built their prosperity by burning fossil fuels for over a century, so they carry a historical responsibility for the atmospheric space that has already been used. Asking a family in a low-income country to remain poor in order to protect a climate damaged by others is clearly unjust.\n\nThe situation is nevertheless more complicated than a simple reduction. Much of the manufacturing that supplies rich consumers takes place in developing economies, so a sudden fall in demand would cost jobs precisely where incomes are lowest. Consumption is also not uniformly harmful; spending on education, healthcare or public transport does relatively little environmental damage compared with air travel and beef.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that developed countries must reduce their material footprint, but the objective should be changing what is consumed rather than merely consuming less. Directing wealth towards low-impact goods and services, while transferring clean technology to poorer nations, would achieve far more than austerity alone.",
