   "prompt": "Some people believe that consumers, not governments, should be responsible for choosing environmentally friendly products. To what extent do you agree or disagree? (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "Intro: 改写题目并表明不同意把责任主要放在消费者身上。",
    "Body 1: 消费者选择的局限 - 信息不透明、漂绿、价格门槛。",
    "Body 2: 政府的优势 - 统一标准、禁止最差产品、生产者责任制。",
    "结论: 消费者可以推动市场，但只有监管能保证底线。"
   ],
   "model": "Shoppers are frequently told that their choices will determine whether companies behave responsibly. Although consumer pressure has some influence, I disagree that responsibility should rest mainly with individuals.\n\nThe first problem is information. Terms such as eco-friendly and natural have no legal meaning in most countries, and a European investigation concluded that over half of environmental claims were misleading. Choosing well therefore requires research that few people have time to do. Price is a further barrier: sustainable options often cost significantly more, so the poorest consumers, who have the smallest environmental impact, are asked to pay the most to improve it.\n\nGovernments, by contrast, can act on the whole market at once. Banning the least efficient appliances, requiring standardised labelling and charging manufacturers for the waste their products create change what is available rather than what is advertised. When the European Union prohibited traditional light bulbs, energy use fell without any consumer needing to compare products. Regulation also creates fair competition, since responsible companies are no longer undercut by those that cut corners.\n\nIn conclusion, consumer choice is a useful signal, but it cannot substitute for rules. The most effective role for individuals may be political rather than commercial: supporting the regulations that remove harmful products from sale altogether, so that the sustainable option becomes the only option.",
