   "model": "Organic farming avoids synthetic pesticides and fertilisers, and several governments now subsidise it despite its lower productivity. I support such policies in a limited form, but I do not believe organic methods should be adopted universally.\n\nThe environmental advantages within organic fields are well established. Studies consistently find around thirty per cent more species on organic farms, including the pollinating insects on which many crops depend. Soil organic matter increases, improving water retention during droughts, and animals kept under organic rules have measurably more space and outdoor access. Reducing pesticide use also protects the health of farm workers, who are exposed far more heavily than consumers.\n\nThe objection, however, is serious. Organic yields are roughly a fifth lower, so meeting the same demand requires additional land. If that land comes from clearing forest, whether locally or abroad, the overall environmental result may be negative. Higher prices also place organic food beyond the reach of many families, which raises questions of fairness when public money is involved.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe governments should support the specific practices that deliver the greatest benefit, such as crop rotation, hedgerow planting and reduced pesticide use, rather than subsidising organic certification as a whole. This approach captures most of the ecological gains while avoiding the yield penalty that makes complete conversion impractical.",
