   "model": "Household waste has become a visible environmental problem, and opinions differ over whether responsibility lies with consumers or with the businesses that supply them.\n\nThere is a genuine case for individual responsibility. Food waste is the largest single component of most household bins, and it results almost entirely from decisions made at home: buying more than is needed, ignoring leftovers and misunderstanding date labels. No supermarket can plan a family's meals. Similarly, choosing a reusable cup or a refill shop is a personal decision, and households that make such changes typically reduce their waste substantially within a few months.\n\nNevertheless, the range of choices available is determined by producers. Consumers cannot buy loose vegetables if none are sold, and they cannot avoid excessive packaging that arrives with an online order. Confusing date labels, which cause enormous quantities of edible food to be discarded, are printed by manufacturers rather than chosen by shoppers. Supermarkets also decide portion sizes and multi-buy offers that actively encourage overbuying.\n\nIn my opinion, businesses bear the greater responsibility because they design the system within which individuals choose. Governments should require refill options, standardised date labelling and reduced packaging, while continuing to educate the public about food storage and planning. Individuals can only select from what is on the shelf; deciding what goes on the shelf is a corporate and political choice.",
