   "model": "Some people argue that constant self-criticism drives success, while others believe that accepting yourself produces better long-term results. In my view, both matter, but acceptance should come first.\n\nThere is clearly some value in being strict with yourself. Students who demand high standards revise more carefully and rarely hand in careless work. Without any internal pressure, it is easy to settle for the first draft of everything.\n\nHowever, harsh self-criticism has a hidden cost. When failure feels like proof of personal worthlessness, people begin to avoid challenges altogether. A learner who is terrified of speaking badly will simply stop speaking, and therefore never improves. Those who accept their limits calmly can look at a poor result, identify the reason, and try again the following week.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe the healthiest approach is to be demanding about the work but forgiving about the person. High standards keep quality up, while self-acceptance keeps us in the game long enough to reach them.",
