  "reading": {
   "words": 254,
   "text": "Anxiety has a bad reputation, but it began as a survival system. When the brain detects a possible threat, it prepares the body to act: the heart beats faster, breathing becomes shallow, and attention narrows to the danger. For an ancestor facing a wild animal, this was extremely useful. The difficulty today is that the same system responds to emails, exams and unanswered messages, none of which can be solved by running away.\n\nBecause the reaction starts in the body, purely mental advice such as stop worrying rarely works. Physical strategies are more reliable. Slow breathing, in which the out-breath is longer than the in-breath, signals to the nervous system that the emergency is over. Walking, cold water on the wrists and simply naming the feeling out loud all reduce the intensity within minutes. None of these techniques removes the problem, but they return enough calm for clear thinking.\n\nThe second stage is to work with the thoughts. Anxious thinking is usually specific in its fear and vague in its evidence: something terrible will happen, but the details are missing. Writing the worry as a full sentence, then asking what would actually happen next, often shrinks it to a manageable size. A useful question is whether the situation is a problem to be solved or a discomfort to be tolerated. Problems deserve a plan; discomfort deserves patience. Most importantly, avoidance feeds anxiety. Each time we skip the phone call or the presentation, relief arrives immediately, and the fear grows slightly larger for next time.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "According to the passage, what physical changes happen when the brain detects a threat?",
     "a": "The heart beats faster, breathing becomes shallow, and attention narrows to the danger.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why does the writer say that advice such as stop worrying rarely works?",
     "a": "Because the reaction starts in the body, so physical strategies are needed rather than purely mental instructions.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main idea of the passage?",
     "a": "Anxiety is a natural survival response that can be managed through physical calming techniques and clearer thinking.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "In the final paragraph, 'avoidance feeds anxiety' most nearly means:",
     "a": "escaping the situation makes the fear stronger next time.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards anxiety?",
     "a": "Practical and non-judgemental; anxiety is treated as a normal mechanism that can be handled, not as a personal fault.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
