  "reading": {
   "words": 258,
   "text": "A boundary is simply a piece of information: this is what I can offer, and this is where I stop. Described that way, it sounds harmless. In practice, many people find boundaries almost impossible to state, because they confuse them with rejection. Saying that you cannot take on a fourth project feels like saying that you do not care about your team. The confusion is understandable, but it is expensive. People without boundaries do not become more generous; they become exhausted, and eventually resentful.\n\nResentment is the clearest signal that a limit has been crossed. It rarely appears when we help someone freely. It appears when we agree to something we did not want, then feel used. This is why boundaries are often more useful to the relationship than to the individual. A friend who always says yes and quietly complains afterwards is harder to trust than one who occasionally says, not this weekend, but call me next week.\n\nStating a boundary well requires very few words. The formula that works in most cultures is short, kind and specific: thank the person, give a clear answer, and offer an alternative if you genuinely have one. Long explanations tend to invite negotiation, since every reason can be argued with. It also helps to remember that other people are allowed to be disappointed. Their reaction is information, not a verdict. Most will simply accept the answer and ask someone else, which is exactly what should happen. The uncomfortable truth is that we usually have far more freedom than we use.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "According to the passage, what happens to people who never set boundaries?",
     "a": "They become exhausted and eventually resentful rather than more generous.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why does the writer say that long explanations are unhelpful?",
     "a": "Because every reason can be argued with, so explaining invites the other person to negotiate.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main message of the passage?",
     "a": "Boundaries are clear information about our limits and they protect relationships as well as individuals.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "In the final paragraph, 'a verdict' most nearly means:",
     "a": "a final judgement about whether you are right or wrong.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards people who struggle to say no?",
     "a": "Sympathetic but firm; the writer understands the fear yet insists the habit is costly.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
