  "reading": {
   "words": 256,
   "text": "Empathy is regularly described as a feeling, but it is more accurately a skill made of several parts. The first is noticing. Before anything can be understood, a change in someone's face, voice or message has to be registered, and this is where most failures occur. We are usually too busy preparing our own reply. The second part is imagining accurately, and the third is responding in a way the other person can actually receive.\n\nThe middle stage causes surprising trouble. Many people assume that empathy means imagining how they themselves would feel in the same situation. This is a reasonable starting point and a poor finishing point, because the other person is not us. A colleague who loves attention will experience public praise very differently from one who dreads it. Skilled listeners therefore ask rather than assume, and they treat their first interpretation as a guess to be checked.\n\nThe response stage is where good intentions are most often wasted. When someone describes a problem, our instinct is to fix it, and advice arrives before the story is finished. Yet research on supportive conversations consistently finds that people feel helped when their experience is acknowledged, not when it is solved. Sentences such as that sounds exhausting or no wonder you were upset do more work than any suggestion. Advice can follow later, and is far more likely to be accepted once the person feels understood. None of this requires deep emotional talent. It requires slowing down, staying curious and resisting the urge to speak first.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "According to the passage, what are the three parts of empathy?",
     "a": "Noticing a change, imagining accurately, and responding in a way the other person can receive.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why is imagining how you would feel described as a poor finishing point?",
     "a": "Because other people react differently, so an assumption based on yourself may be completely wrong.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main idea of the passage?",
     "a": "Empathy is a practical skill of noticing, checking assumptions and acknowledging feelings rather than an inborn emotion.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "In the last paragraph, 'wasted' most nearly means:",
     "a": "used without producing any useful effect.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards giving advice quickly?",
     "a": "Critical; the writer sees it as well-meant but usually less helpful than acknowledgement.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
