  "reading": {
   "words": 263,
   "text": "Guilt has a bad reputation among people who have never needed it. In moderation, it is one of the most socially useful emotions we possess. It appears when our behaviour conflicts with our own values, and it pushes us towards repair: an apology, a returned favour, a changed habit. Societies without any sense of guilt would be unpleasant places to live. The problem is not guilt itself but guilt that has lost its function.\n\nPsychologists draw a sharp line between guilt and shame. Guilt says I did something bad; shame says I am something bad. The first points at an action that can be corrected, while the second points at a self that cannot. This distinction explains why guilt often produces apologies and shame often produces hiding. People who feel deeply ashamed frequently avoid the person they have harmed, which looks like indifference but is closer to panic.\n\nUseful guilt is therefore specific, proportional and temporary. It identifies what was done, suggests what can be repaired, and then ends. When guilt continues for years, it usually means one of three things: the harm was never acknowledged out loud, the repair was never attempted, or the standard being applied was impossible in the first place. Working parents provide the clearest modern example. Many feel constant guilt not because they neglect their children, but because they measure themselves against a fantasy in which nobody is ever tired. In such cases the honest response is not more effort. It is a more realistic standard, and permission to stop paying for a crime that was never committed.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "According to the passage, what useful function does guilt have?",
     "a": "It appears when behaviour conflicts with our values and pushes us towards repair such as apologising or changing a habit.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why do people who feel deep shame often avoid the person they have harmed?",
     "a": "Because shame attacks the whole self, so hiding feels safer than facing the situation; it resembles panic rather than indifference.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main idea of the passage?",
     "a": "Guilt is valuable when it is specific and temporary, but harmful when it turns into shame or is based on impossible standards.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "In the final sentence, 'a crime that was never committed' refers to:",
     "a": "guilt about failing to meet an unrealistic standard rather than any actual wrongdoing.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards working parents who feel guilty?",
     "a": "Sympathetic; the writer believes their guilt often comes from impossible expectations rather than real neglect.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
