  "reading": {
   "words": 265,
   "text": "Comparison is not a character flaw; it is how the mind measures. Without any external reference, we would have no way of knowing whether our salary is fair, our work good enough, or our progress reasonable. The trouble is that the human comparison system was designed for villages of a hundred people and now operates on a planet of eight billion, most of whom present only their best moments.\n\nTwo details make modern comparison particularly painful. The first is direction. We overwhelmingly compare upwards, towards those who have more, and rarely downwards, towards those who have less. The second is selection. We compare our complete inner experience, including doubts, debts and bad mornings, with other people's edited exterior. The result is a competition that cannot be won, because we are matching everything we know about ourselves against everything they have chosen to show.\n\nThere is, however, a version of comparison that helps. Psychologists distinguish between envy that says I want that and therefore I hate you, and admiration that says I want that and therefore I will find out how. The second reaction treats another person as evidence that something is possible, and it usually produces questions rather than resentment. Changing the benchmark also helps considerably. Measuring today against your own performance last year gives accurate, useful information, since the circumstances are genuinely comparable. Some people go further and deliberately limit the comparisons available to them, unfollowing accounts that reliably produce that hollow feeling. This is not weakness. It is simply recognising that attention, like any resource, should be spent where it does some good.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "According to the passage, what two details make modern comparison especially painful?",
     "a": "We mostly compare upwards, and we compare our full inner experience with other people's edited exterior.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why does the writer say the competition cannot be won?",
     "a": "Because we match everything we know about ourselves against only what others choose to show.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main idea of the passage?",
     "a": "Comparison is natural but often distorted; it becomes useful when redirected towards admiration and self-referenced benchmarks.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "In the last paragraph, 'that hollow feeling' most nearly means:",
     "a": "the empty, dissatisfied feeling produced by seeing other people's apparent success.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards unfollowing certain accounts?",
     "a": "Approving; the writer sees it as a sensible use of limited attention rather than avoidance.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
