   "model": "There is ongoing debate about whether employers should take responsibility for the mental wellbeing of their staff. In my opinion, companies have a genuine duty, though it has clear limits.\n\nThe argument in favour is straightforward. Many causes of stress are created at work: unrealistic deadlines, unclear instructions and messages sent late at night. Since the organisation produces these pressures, it should also reduce them. There is a financial case too, as staff who are exhausted make more errors and leave more often, which is expensive to repair.\n\nOn the other hand, critics reasonably warn about privacy. Employees may not want managers asking about their personal lives, and companies are not qualified to provide therapy. A poorly designed wellness programme can even feel like surveillance.\n\nMy conclusion is that responsibility should be defined by control. Employers should manage workload, train managers and guarantee genuine rest, because these are within their power. Treatment for serious conditions belongs to health services, not to human resources departments.",
