   "model": "It is sometimes argued that constantly trying to keep other people happy makes honest communication impossible. I largely agree with this claim, though I would distinguish between politeness and dishonesty.\n\nWhen someone agrees with everything, their opinion loses its value. A manager who receives only positive feedback cannot identify weaknesses in a plan, and problems are discovered far too late, usually when they are expensive. In teams where disagreement is unwelcome, mistakes are repeated for years because nobody is willing to name them.\n\nNevertheless, consideration for others is not the same as deception. In many cultures, criticism is expressed indirectly out of respect, and this can work perfectly well if both sides understand the code. Choosing a gentle phrase is very different from pretending to agree.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe the problem is not kindness but the suppression of genuine views. Honest communication requires that people state their real position, even if they do so tactfully; without that, politeness gradually becomes a way of hiding the truth.",
