   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目并表明同意。",
    "主体1：支持理由——GDP 无法反映健康、孤独与工作压力，幸福数据可指导政策。",
    "主体2：反方顾虑与回应——幸福难以量化且可能被操纵，但可用标准化调查改善。",
    "结论：应作为经济指标的补充而非替代。"
   ],
   "model": "It has been suggested that governments should measure national happiness alongside economic performance. I agree with this proposal, provided the data is used carefully.\n\nEconomic growth alone gives an incomplete picture. A country can record rising output while its citizens sleep badly, work longer hours and see their friends less often. Well-being surveys reveal these problems earlier, and several governments have used such data to justify investment in mental health services and public parks, decisions that pure economic figures would not have supported.\n\nCritics reasonably argue that happiness is subjective and difficult to compare between cultures. There is also a risk that governments would present favourable results and ignore inconvenient ones. However, the same criticisms apply to unemployment statistics, which are nevertheless collected using agreed standards, and independent research bodies can reduce political interference.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe well-being should be measured as a complement to economic indicators rather than a replacement for them. Together they show not only how much a country produces, but whether that production actually improves the lives of its people.",
