   "text": "Every city has streets that have been written off. Shops close, buildings stand empty, and the people who stay are told that nothing can be done. Yet some of these places recover, and the reasons are rarely mysterious. Where regeneration works, it is almost always because residents were treated as partners rather than as a problem to be solved. Where it fails, decisions were made in an office far away and announced when it was too late to change them.\n\nThe clearest example is what happens to derelict space. A council can sell an empty site to a developer and watch expensive flats appear, or it can lease the same land to a grassroots group for a workshop, a market or a garden. Both options bring money, but only the second keeps the benefit inside the neighbourhood. Genuine public consultation costs time, and it slows projects down, yet schemes that skip it are frequently delayed anyway by objections and legal challenges.\n\nThe uncomfortable side of renewal is displacement. When a district becomes attractive, rents rise, and the families who survived the difficult years are pushed out by newcomers who can pay more. Planners now try to reduce this effect by protecting affordable housing and by supporting existing small businesses. Nobody has found a perfect answer. What is clear is that a neighbourhood revived without its original residents has not really been rebuilt at all. For that reason, several cities now measure success differently. Instead of counting new businesses or rising property values, they ask how many families who lived in the district ten years ago are still there today. It is an uncomfortable statistic, and precisely for that reason it is a useful one.",
