  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Nowadays people photograph almost every moment of their lives. Some think this improves memory, while others believe it prevents people from truly experiencing events. Discuss both views and give your opinion.",
   "outline": [
    "开头：描述随手拍照的现象与争议",
    "主体一：支持——影像帮助回忆、记录家庭历史、分享情感",
    "主体二：反对——隔着屏幕体验、注意力分散、照片很少再看",
    "结论：适度拍摄，先经历再记录"
   ],
   "model": "Smartphones have turned ordinary life into a continuous photo session. Some people welcome this, arguing that it protects our memories, while others claim it separates us from the moment itself.\n\nThere is clear value in recording events. Photographs preserve details that the mind quickly loses, such as how a grandparent's kitchen looked or how small a child once was, and families keep these images for decades. Sharing pictures also allows relatives abroad to feel part of celebrations they cannot attend.\n\nOn the other hand, constant photography changes behaviour. At concerts, audiences watch through a screen rather than with their own eyes, and travellers rush between famous sites collecting evidence of a visit instead of enjoying it. Research suggests that people who photograph everything often remember less, partly because they pay attention to the camera rather than the experience, and thousands of images are never looked at again.\n\nIn my opinion the problem is quantity, not photography itself. Taking two or three considered pictures and then putting the phone away offers the benefits of both positions: a lasting record and an undisturbed experience. Cameras should serve our memories rather than replace them.",
   "checklist": [
    "双方观点均有独立段落",
    "有具体场景例子（演唱会、旅行）",
    "结论提出折中并回应题目",
    "动名词与不定式搭配正确",
    "衔接自然，字数 150-200"
   ]
  },
