  "reading": {
   "words": 300,
   "text": "Every culture on earth tells stories, and researchers believe the habit is older than writing, farming or cities. The reason is practical rather than romantic. A list of facts is difficult to remember, while the same information inside a story survives for generations, because our minds are built to follow people, problems and consequences.\n\nThe structure is remarkably consistent. Someone wants something, an obstacle appears, and the situation changes them. Whether the setting is a village legend or a modern business presentation, listeners wait for that pattern, and they become uncomfortable when a speaker offers information without it. This explains why a talk full of accurate statistics can be forgotten within an hour, while a two-minute anecdote about one customer is repeated in the office for weeks.\n\nDetail is the second secret. Beginners describe events in general terms, saying that the journey was difficult, whereas skilled storytellers mention the broken zip on a rucksack or the smell of wet cardboard. Specific details do not slow a story down; they make the listener believe it. At the same time, restraint matters. Too much background produces impatience, and audiences forgive almost anything except a story that takes too long to begin. The final element is honesty. Stories that end with the speaker looking heroic rarely resonate, because listeners recognise self-promotion instantly. A moment of failure, admitted simply, creates far more trust. Perhaps that is the real lesson: storytelling is not a technique for decorating information but a way of showing others that you understand what the experience actually felt like.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "Why is information easier to remember inside a story?",
     "a": "Because minds naturally follow people, problems and consequences.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why can a talk full of statistics be forgotten quickly?",
     "a": "Because it lacks the want-obstacle-change pattern that listeners expect.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main point of the passage?",
     "a": "Stories work because of structure, concrete detail and honesty, not decoration.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "The word 'restraint' in paragraph 3 means:",
     "a": "holding back / not saying too much.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's view of self-promoting stories?",
     "a": "Critical: audiences distrust them and prefer admitted failure.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
