   "prompt": "Many animal and plant species are becoming extinct because of human activity. Some people think governments should spend money protecting them, while others believe this money is needed elsewhere. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目，说明物种灭绝已成全球问题。",
    "主体一：反对花钱保护的理由——资源有限，人类需求优先。",
    "主体二：支持保护的理由——生态系统相互依赖，物种消失影响农业与医药。",
    "结论：主张保护成本远低于生态崩溃的代价，应优先保护栖息地。"
   ],
   "model": "Human activity is driving many species towards extinction, and there is disagreement about whether public money should be used to prevent this.\n\nSome people argue that conservation is a luxury. Governments face urgent demands in housing, healthcare and education, and protecting a rare frog can seem unimportant beside these needs. They also point out that species have always died out naturally, long before humans existed.\n\nOthers reply that this view is short-sighted. Species do not exist alone; they form ecosystems that provide food, clean water and stable soil. Bees pollinate crops, and many modern medicines were first discovered in wild plants. When a species disappears, the practical loss is often discovered too late.\n\nPersonally, I find the second argument more convincing. Protecting habitats is usually far cheaper than repairing damaged ecosystems afterwards, and much of the work involves preventing harm rather than spending large sums. Governments could also involve local communities, who often protect land more effectively than distant officials.\n\nIn conclusion, although funds are limited, I believe conservation should be treated as a long-term investment rather than an optional expense.",
