   "text": "We have detailed maps of the surface of Mars, yet most of our own seabed remains unmapped in any real detail. The reason is simple physics. Radio waves and light do not travel far through water, so satellites are almost useless below the surface. Anything we want to know about the deep must be measured by sound, or by sending equipment down into crushing pressure and permanent darkness. That is slow work, and it is expensive.\n\nWhat we have found so far suggests how much is still missing. Every serious expedition to the deep returns with species that have never been described. Creatures there survive without sunlight, feeding on chemicals that leak from cracks in the ocean floor. Communities of tube worms and pale crabs live around these vents in water hot enough to melt lead, kept liquid only by the pressure above them. Before 1977, almost no biologist believed such places could exist.\n\nMeanwhile the parts we do understand are changing fast. Coral reefs, which grow only a few centimetres a year, are bleaching as the water warms. Plastic never fully breaks down, so it gathers in currents far from any city. The ocean absorbs much of the heat and carbon we produce, quietly protecting us from the worst of climate change. That service is not free, and the bill is arriving in the form of a fragile, acidifying sea. Warmer water also holds less oxygen, which pushes fish towards the poles and empties seas that supported fishing communities for centuries. None of this is happening quietly, yet the changes take place out of sight. It is difficult to protect something that most people will never look at directly.",
