   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目，说明城市绿化立法的争议。",
    "主体一：优势——降温、净化空气、改善心理健康与房产价值。",
    "主体二：不足——成本高、维护困难、根系损坏道路与管线。",
    "结论：优势明显大于劣势，但需选对树种与地点。"
   ],
   "model": "In recent years several governments have considered making urban tree planting a legal requirement. In my view, the benefits of such a policy clearly outweigh the drawbacks, provided that it is carried out sensibly.\n\nThe advantages are considerable. Trees lower street temperatures by several degrees in summer, which reduces both electricity use and heat-related illness. They also absorb dust and noise, and research consistently shows that residents of greener streets report lower stress. In addition, shaded neighbourhoods tend to attract more pedestrians, which helps local shops.\n\nThere are, however, genuine disadvantages. Planting is expensive, and the real cost lies in decades of pruning, watering and eventual removal. Roots can lift pavements and damage underground pipes, and unsuitable species may drop heavy branches or produce pollen that affects people with allergies.\n\nThese problems, though, are arguments for careful planning rather than for doing nothing. Choosing native species with predictable root systems, and planting them where there is space below ground, prevents most of the damage described above.\n\nOverall, I believe compulsory planting is justified, because the long-term health and climate benefits are far greater than the maintenance costs involved.",
