   "model": "Scientists are increasingly expected to explain their work to non-specialists, but there is disagreement about how far complex ideas should be simplified.\n\nThose in favour argue that public understanding is necessary in a democracy. Citizens vote on policies concerning energy, vaccination and climate, and they cannot judge these issues if the underlying science is inaccessible. Clear explanation also encourages young people to study science, which benefits society in the long run.\n\nCritics, however, point out that simplification often distorts. When uncertainty is removed to make a headline clearer, readers may believe a result is final when it is still being tested. Words such as quantum are then borrowed by advertisers, and the public ends up more confused than before.\n\nIn my view, the solution is not to avoid explanation but to explain more honestly. A good communicator can describe what a study shows, what it does not show, and how confident researchers actually are. This takes more space than a headline, yet it builds trust rather than destroying it.\n\nTo conclude, science should certainly be made accessible, provided that simplification includes the limits of the evidence as well as the findings themselves.",
   "checklist": [
    "✔ 双方论点各成一段并互相对照",
    "✔ 个人观点具体可行，而非笼统",
    "✔ 使用 those in favour, critics, in my view, to conclude",
    "✔ 至少两个例子（投票议题 / 广告用词）",
    "✔ 全文围绕题目关键词展开，无跑题"
   ]
