   "prompt": "Many famous natural sites are being damaged by large numbers of tourists. Some people think visitor numbers should be strictly limited. Do you agree or disagree? (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目并表明基本同意限流。",
    "主体一：支持理由——踩踏侵蚀、垃圾与当地资源压力，恢复代价高昂。",
    "主体二：反对意见与回应——限流影响当地经济与公平性，可用预约与浮动票价解决。",
    "结论：主张有管理的限流而非完全开放或关闭。"
   ],
   "model": "Several of the world's best-known natural sites now receive more visitors in a week than they once did in a year. I agree that numbers should be limited, although the way limits are applied is crucially important.\n\nThe case for restriction is straightforward. Footpaths widen and erode when thousands of people walk the same route daily, and fragile surfaces such as coral, cave floors and alpine plants recover extremely slowly, if at all. Small communities also struggle when water and waste systems designed for a village must serve a crowd.\n\nOpponents argue that restrictions are unfair, since they can make access a privilege for those who can afford high prices, and local businesses may lose income. These concerns are reasonable, but they are arguments about method rather than principle. Free timed booking systems, seasonal quotas and reduced rates for residents can protect both the site and the local economy.\n\nIn my opinion, the alternative is worse. A landscape that is destroyed generates no income at all, and repairing damage usually costs far more than preventing it.\n\nIn conclusion, I support carefully managed limits, because they protect irreplaceable places while still allowing reasonable public access.",
