   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目，指出缺水已影响多国。",
    "主体一：原因——人口与农业需水增长、灌溉浪费、污染与冰川融水减少。",
    "主体二：措施——滴灌技术、修复管网漏损、合理水价、废水回用与跨境协议。",
    "结论：需求管理比新建水坝更有效。"
   ],
   "model": "Access to fresh water is becoming increasingly difficult in many regions, and the reasons are both natural and human.\n\nThe main cause is rising demand. Growing populations require more food, and agriculture already consumes about seventy per cent of the fresh water used worldwide. Much of this is wasted through open channels and flood irrigation, which lose enormous quantities to evaporation. Pollution reduces the usable supply further, since rivers contaminated by industry or sewage cannot easily be used for drinking. In mountainous regions, shrinking glaciers are also reducing the reliable summer flow on which millions depend.\n\nFortunately, effective responses exist. Drip irrigation delivers water directly to plant roots and can cut agricultural use dramatically, as Israeli farms have demonstrated. Repairing leaking pipes is unglamorous but highly effective, since some city networks lose a third of their supply before it reaches a tap. Charging realistic prices for industrial and agricultural water discourages waste, provided that basic domestic supply remains affordable. Treated wastewater can safely be reused for irrigation and industry, and neighbouring countries sharing a river need formal agreements to prevent conflict.\n\nIn conclusion, shortages result largely from wasteful use rather than an absolute lack of water, so managing demand offers greater benefit than simply building more reservoirs.",
