   "model": "In several regions, once-productive farmland is gradually becoming desert, and human activity is largely responsible.\n\nOvergrazing is one of the clearest causes. When too many animals are kept on dry grassland, they remove the plant cover that holds soil in place, and wind then carries away the fertile upper layer. Cutting trees for fuel and construction has a similar effect, since roots and shade both protect the ground. Irrigation, surprisingly, can also cause damage: when water is applied heavily in hot climates, it evaporates and leaves salt behind, eventually poisoning the soil. Reduced rainfall in a warming climate makes all these processes worse.\n\nPractical solutions are well established. Limiting herd sizes and rotating grazing land allows vegetation to recover, though this requires agreement among local herders. Planting belts of drought-resistant trees reduces wind erosion, and China's large-scale planting programmes have slowed the advance of sand in some provinces. Replacing flood irrigation with drip systems prevents salt accumulation, and restoring native plants is usually more successful than importing foreign species.\n\nIn conclusion, desertification is caused mainly by pressure on fragile land, and reversing it requires sustained investment together with the cooperation of the communities who farm there.",
