   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目，说明光污染现状。",
    "主体一：为何是问题——干扰野生动物与人类睡眠、浪费能源、文化与科学损失。",
    "主体二：对策——向下照射的灯具、夜间调暗、社区暗夜保护区、公众教育。",
    "结论：光污染是最容易逆转的污染之一。"
   ],
   "model": "In most large cities today, only a handful of stars remain visible, and I consider this a genuine problem rather than a minor aesthetic complaint.\n\nThe effects extend well beyond astronomy. Excessive lighting disrupts wildlife: migrating birds are drawn off course by illuminated buildings, and insect populations, already declining, are strongly affected by street lamps. Human health is involved as well, since bright light at night interferes with sleep patterns. There is also considerable waste, because light directed upwards illuminates nothing but the sky and still consumes electricity. Finally, a cultural loss occurs when a generation grows up having never seen the Milky Way.\n\nFortunately, this form of pollution is unusually easy to reduce. Simply fitting shields so that lamps direct light downwards immediately cuts the glow without reducing safety on the ground. Councils can dim or switch off lighting in industrial areas after midnight, and warmer-coloured lights disturb both people and animals less than harsh white ones. Several regions have created dark sky reserves, which have proved surprisingly popular with tourists.\n\nIn conclusion, light pollution damages wildlife, health and heritage, yet unlike most environmental problems it disappears the moment the lights are correctly adjusted.",
