   "prompt": "Some people think that governments should only fund scientific research with clear practical benefits. To what extent do you agree or disagree? (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目并表明不同意为主。",
    "主体一：承认实用研究重要——医疗、农业与能源需求紧迫，纳税人有权要求回报。",
    "主体二：反驳——基础研究成果无法预测，如相对论催生卫星导航，电磁学起初也无用途。",
    "结论：主张以基础研究为主的合理比例投入。"
   ],
   "model": "It is sometimes argued that public money should support only research with obvious applications. I disagree with this position, although I understand why it appeals to taxpayers.\n\nThe case for practical research is not unreasonable. Health systems need better treatments, agriculture needs more resilient crops, and energy systems must be rebuilt quickly. When budgets are limited, funding a project with an immediate benefit seems more responsible than supporting abstract theory.\n\nThe difficulty is that this argument assumes we can predict which discoveries will prove useful, and history shows repeatedly that we cannot. When Einstein described how gravity affects time, the work had no conceivable application; today satellite navigation would drift by kilometres each day without those corrections. Early research on electricity and magnetism was regarded as pure curiosity, and the study of bacterial defence mechanisms, funded as basic biology, eventually produced modern gene-editing tools.\n\nThere is also a practical consideration. Applied research depends on a supply of trained scientists, and these people are educated through exactly the kind of fundamental work that appears useless at the time.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe governments should continue funding curiosity-driven science, since the most valuable applications have consistently emerged from research that no one could justify in advance.",
