   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目，说明载人火星计划的争议。",
    "主体一：反对方——风险高、耗资巨大、地球问题更紧迫、机器人可替代。",
    "主体二：支持方——推动技术与国际合作、激励科学教育、长期分散风险。",
    "结论：主张先完成机器人取样与技术验证，再考虑载人。"
   ],
   "model": "Plans to send astronauts to Mars attract both enthusiasm and strong criticism, and the arguments on each side deserve consideration.\n\nOpponents emphasise cost and danger. A crewed mission would last around two years, and the crew could not return early in an emergency. The sums involved would fund hospitals or climate projects that produce immediate benefits, and critics argue that robotic missions already collect excellent scientific data at a fraction of the price and with no risk to life.\n\nSupporters respond that difficult goals produce unexpected returns. Developing closed life-support systems, radiation shielding and reliable recycling technology would benefit medicine and environmental engineering on Earth. Such projects also encourage international cooperation and attract young people into science, which has lasting economic value. Some add that establishing a second location for humanity is a sensible long-term precaution.\n\nMy own position is cautious support with a clear sequence. The immediate priority should be returning rock samples robotically and demonstrating that oxygen and fuel can be produced on Mars. Only once these steps succeed does risking a crew become defensible.\n\nIn conclusion, while I understand the objections, I believe a carefully staged programme is justified, provided that human flight follows rather than replaces robotic preparation.",
