   "prompt": "Some people believe that mining the ocean floor for valuable metals should be banned until its effects are fully understood. To what extent do you agree or disagree? (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "引言：改写题目，说明深海采矿刚刚起步。",
    "主体一：支持暂缓——生态恢复极慢、物种未被记录、损害不可逆。",
    "主体二：反对暂缓——电池金属需求迫切，陆地采矿同样破坏且涉及劳工问题。",
    "结论：赞成有期限的暂停并同时投入回收技术。"
   ],
   "model": "Interest in extracting metals from the ocean floor has grown rapidly, and there is serious debate about whether it should proceed. I agree that a pause is justified until the consequences are better understood.\n\nThe strongest argument for waiting concerns recovery. Studies of experimental mining sites show that tracks made decades ago remain visible and that the animal communities living in that sediment have not returned. Many of the species involved have never been formally described, so we would be removing habitats before knowing what they contain. Unlike a forest, the deep seabed cannot be replanted.\n\nThere are, however, genuine counterarguments. Demand for battery metals is rising steeply, and land-based mining causes deforestation, water pollution and, in some regions, serious abuses of workers. Refusing to consider the seabed may simply shift the damage somewhere less visible.\n\nThese points are important, but they support delay rather than dismissal. A temporary moratorium would allow proper environmental assessment while investment is directed towards recycling, which currently recovers only a small proportion of the metals already in circulation.\n\nIn conclusion, I support a time-limited ban, because a decision that cannot be reversed should not be taken while the evidence remains this incomplete.",
