   "words": 288,
   "text": "The story of renewable energy over the last fifteen years is mainly a story about cost. In 2010, electricity from solar panels was among the most expensive available, and it was supported by subsidies that many economists considered wasteful. Those subsidies created a market, the market created scale, and manufacturing costs fell by roughly ninety per cent. Solar is now the cheapest source of new electricity in most of the world, which almost no forecaster predicted.\n\nThe remaining problem is not cost but timing. Wind and solar are intermittent: they produce when conditions allow rather than when demand requires. A grid dominated by them therefore needs something to fill the gaps. Batteries handle a few hours very well and have become dramatically cheaper, but storing energy from a windy autumn until a still, cold January remains unsolved at reasonable cost. Most engineers expect a mixture of solutions, including long-distance transmission lines that move power from wherever the wind is currently blowing.\n\nThere is also a materials question that receives less attention. Turbines, panels and batteries require copper, lithium and rare metals, and mining these has environmental and social costs of its own. This does not undermine the case for renewables, since fossil fuel extraction is far larger in volume and produces continuous emissions. It does mean, however, that recycling and efficient design deserve as much investment as generation itself. A battery that lasts fifteen years instead of eight halves the demand for new material, and recovering lithium from old packs is already technically possible. The industry has grown so recently that most of these products are still in use, so the recycling problem has not yet arrived. It will, and preparing now is considerably cheaper than reacting later.",
