  "reading": {
   "words": 284,
   "text": "A widely cited study from MIT examined how true and false stories travel on social media. The researchers expected some difference; what they found was startling. False stories reached far more people, and they did so about six times faster. The explanation had little to do with technology. False stories were simply more surprising, and surprise is what makes a human finger tap \"share\".\n\nThis creates a difficult environment for ordinary readers. Most misinformation is not an obvious lie. It is a real photograph from a different year, a genuine statistic stripped of its context, or an accurate quotation with the qualifying sentence removed. Because each element is technically true, the usual advice to \"check the facts\" is not quite enough. What has to be checked is the framing.\n\nProfessional fact-checkers use a technique that ordinary readers can copy. Instead of studying a suspicious page closely, they leave it immediately and search for what other sources say about it. This lateral approach takes less than a minute and is far more reliable than examining a website's design, which tells you nothing except that someone can afford a designer. Three further questions help: who benefits if I believe this, what evidence is offered, and is the headline actually supported by the article? The last question is the most revealing, since headlines are frequently written by someone who did not write the piece and whose job is to attract attention rather than to summarise accurately. Being sceptical in this sense does not mean believing nothing. It means holding a claim lightly until it has been verified, and being equally suspicious of stories that flatter your existing opinions.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "According to the passage, how much faster did false stories spread in the MIT study?",
     "a": "About six times faster than true stories.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why is the advice to 'check the facts' insufficient?",
     "a": "Because much misinformation uses technically true elements presented in a misleading frame, so the framing must be checked.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main idea of the passage?",
     "a": "Misinformation spreads because it is surprising, so readers need practical verification habits such as lateral reading.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "In the third paragraph, 'lateral' most nearly means:",
     "a": "moving sideways — here, checking other sources rather than the page itself.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards scepticism?",
     "a": "Positive but balanced; the writer wants readers to suspend judgement rather than disbelieve everything.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
