  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people believe that regularly reviewing past mistakes is the best way to improve, while others think it is better to look forward and not dwell on the past. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "Intro: paraphrase and argue for structured review rather than endless rumination.",
    "Body 1: reviewing reveals patterns and prevents repeated errors.",
    "Body 2: excessive focus on the past can damage confidence and delay action.",
    "Conclusion: brief, regular, written review combines both benefits."
   ],
   "model": "People disagree about whether we should study our past errors closely or concentrate on what comes next. In my view, review is valuable when it is brief and structured, but harmful when it becomes repetitive worry.\n\nThose who favour reflection argue convincingly that experience teaches nothing by itself. A student who receives the same feedback three times without examining it will make the same mistake in the final exam. Professional fields have accepted this: pilots and surgeons hold formal debriefs precisely because memory is unreliable and patterns are invisible in the moment.\n\nOn the other hand, dwelling on failure has real costs. People who replay past mistakes at night lose sleep and confidence without gaining information, since the twentieth review of an event produces nothing that the first did not. There is also the danger of hesitation: someone who fears repeating an error may avoid attempting anything difficult.\n\nI therefore believe the distinction lies in the method. A fixed fifteen minutes each week, with written answers to specific questions, produces insight and then closes the subject. Unstructured worry has no ending and no output.\n\nIn conclusion, we should review the past deliberately rather than endlessly, and then direct our attention firmly forward.",
   "checklist": [
    "✔ 首段改写题目并给出立场",
    "✔ 双方观点各成一段",
    "✔ 具体例子（学生反馈 / 飞行员复盘 / 夜里反刍）",
    "✔ 使用 on the other hand, therefore, in conclusion 衔接",
    "✔ 结论区分结构化复盘与无休止焦虑"
   ]
  },
