  "reading": {
   "words": 283,
   "text": "Charles Handy, writing about organisations, described what he called the second curve. Any product, career or company follows roughly the same shape: a slow difficult beginning, a period of rapid growth, a peak, and then decline. The instinct is to start something new once decline is obvious. Handy's argument was that by then it is too late, because a failing organisation has neither the money nor the confidence to invest. The second curve must begin near the top of the first, when everything appears to be going well and there is no obvious reason to change.\n\nThis is psychologically very difficult. Nobody wants to disrupt success, and colleagues will reasonably ask why resources are being spent on an unproven idea while the current business is thriving. There is also a personal version. A teacher at the height of her reputation has every incentive to continue exactly as she is, and it feels irrational to start studying something new in the evenings.\n\nYet the examples are consistent. Photographic film companies that waited for digital sales to fall disappeared; those that began earlier survived in other forms. Individually, people who change career successfully in their forties almost always started preparing while still employed, using transferable skills rather than beginning from nothing. The two curves overlap for a period, which is uncomfortable and expensive in time. Handy's conclusion was blunt: the best moment to begin something new is when you least feel the need to, and the price of comfort is that you notice the necessity only when the options have narrowed.",
   "q": [
    {
     "q": "According to the passage, when should the second curve begin?",
     "a": "Near the top of the first curve, while things still appear to be going well.",
     "type": "细节"
    },
    {
     "q": "Why does the writer say starting during decline is too late?",
     "a": "Because a failing organisation lacks both the money and the confidence needed to invest in something new.",
     "type": "推断"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the main idea of the passage?",
     "a": "Renewal must start during success, not after decline, even though this feels unnecessary at the time.",
     "type": "主旨"
    },
    {
     "q": "In the last paragraph, 'blunt' most nearly means:",
     "a": "direct and honest, without softening the message.",
     "type": "词义"
    },
    {
     "q": "What is the writer's attitude towards comfort?",
     "a": "Critical; comfort is presented as the reason people delay change until their options have narrowed.",
     "type": "态度"
    }
   ]
  },
