  "writing": {
   "prompt": "Some people believe that people should change their career at least once in their working life, while others think it is better to stay in one profession. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. (250 words)",
   "outline": [
    "Intro: paraphrase and argue change should be possible but not compulsory.",
    "Body 1: staying builds deep expertise, seniority and stability.",
    "Body 2: changing prevents stagnation and protects against obsolete skills.",
    "Conclusion: readiness to change matters more than change itself."
   ],
   "model": "Opinions differ over whether a single profession or several is preferable. In my view, people should remain capable of changing direction, though a change is not always necessary.\n\nThose who stay in one field gain considerable advantages. Genuine expertise takes many years, and surgeons, lawyers and engineers become valuable precisely because they have accumulated experience that cannot be acquired quickly. Long service also brings seniority, pension security and professional networks, all of which are lost when someone begins again at the bottom.\n\nOn the other hand, remaining in one role can be risky. Technology is eliminating tasks that once seemed permanent, and a specialist whose skills become obsolete at fifty may find no equivalent position. Long careers can also produce quiet stagnation, in which someone repeats the same year twenty times without developing.\n\nI therefore believe the important quality is readiness rather than movement. People should maintain transferable skills, study occasionally and keep contacts outside their own industry, so that change is a choice rather than an emergency.\n\nIn conclusion, changing career is not obligatory, but being able to change is increasingly essential in an economy where few jobs remain unaltered for a lifetime.",
   "checklist": [
    "✔ 首段改写题目并给出立场",
    "✔ 双方观点各成一段",
    "✔ 具体例子（专业积累 / 技能过时风险）",
    "✔ 使用 on the other hand, therefore, in conclusion 衔接",
    "✔ 结论强调「随时可变的能力」，无新论点"
   ]
  },
